    "task": "# Year 12 Physics M5 V20: Kepler's laws. How do Kepler's laws describe circular and elliptical orbits? (design)",
    "causalExplanation": "About four hundred years ago, Johannes Kepler worked through twenty years of careful measurements of Mars, made by Tycho Brahe without a telescope. No circle fitted them, however he adjusted it. What fitted was an ellipse. Kepler summed up planetary motion in three laws. They describe how planets move, and Newton's gravity later explained why. Kepler's first law: each planet moves on an ellipse, with the Sun at one focus. An ellipse has two special points called foci. From any point on it, the two distances to the foci always add to the same total. The Sun sits at one focus, and the other is just empty space. Half the longest width is called the semi-major axis, a. The closest point to the Sun is the perihelion, and the furthest is the aphelion. Squash the foci together, and the ellipse becomes a circle, where a is just the radius. Kepler's second law: a line from the Sun to the planet sweeps out equal areas in equal times. Near the Sun, that line is short, so to sweep the same area in a month, the planet has to cover a longer stretch of its path. Far away, the line is long, and a short stretch does the job. So a planet moves fastest at perihelion, and slowest at aphelion.",
